            "image": "forwilma/images/day18_20260628_154837.jpg",
            "captions": {
                "linkedin": "Boundaries Aren't Barriers\n\nI used to think setting limits meant building walls around my kids' lives—keeping them safe by keeping them separate from everything I worried might hurt them. Then one day at the kitchen table, my daughter showed me something she'd learned on YouTube Kids while making slime. Not just how-to steps, but why certain ingredients worked together. She was excited, engaged, learning things I never thought to teach her.\n\nThe boundary we set—that \"kids\" content only—didn't stop her curiosity. It channeled it safely into exactly what seven-year-olds should be exploring.\n\nHere’s the thing we often forget: Boundaries aren't barriers—they're guardrails. They keep kids moving forward without careening off cliffs they can't see yet. The internet has incredible stuff, yes, but also predatory spaces, misinformation, age-inappropriate content disguised as games, and endless scrolling loops designed to hijack developing brains.\n\nResearch shows parents worry most about screen time quantity, but quality matters more. A 20-minute conversation about something cool your kid discovered? That beats three hours of passive consumption any day.\n\nSo where do you start?\nAge-appropriate content filters aren't cages; they're training wheels\nTime limits create space for other things—not deprivation, but balance\nConversations about what they're seeing build critical thinking skills\n\nThe goal isn't control—it's confidence. Confidence that your kids know how to navigate tricky situations, ask questions without shame, and come to you first because boundaries have always felt supportive, not restrictive.\n\nWhat's one boundary you've set that surprised you by opening up conversation instead of shutting it down?\n\n#DigitalParenting #ScreenTimeTips #FamilyTech\n\n#DigitalGuardian #DigitalParenting #DigitalSafety #ParentingTips",
                "bluesky": "Boundaries Aren't Barriers\n\nI used to think setting limits meant building walls around my kids' lives—keeping them safe by keeping them separate from everything I worried might hurt them. ...\n\nWant to read more?... check out my LinkedIn"
            },
